<Layout
  title="EmbeddingGemma 2 in DigUp — On-Device Multimodal Search"
  description="DigUp runs Google DeepMind’s EmbeddingGemma 2 (~740M, ~865 MB Q8 via llama.cpp) locally so one query can rank images, PDF pages, audio, and video in a shared 768-d space."
  canonicalPath="/embeddinggemma-2/"
>
  <main class="wrap">
    <header class="page-hero">
      <p class="meta-line"><span class="badge">Model</span> · Unofficial DigUp angle</p>
      <h1>How DigUp uses EmbeddingGemma 2</h1>
      <p class="lead">DigUp’s search quality comes from Google DeepMind’s EmbeddingGemma 2, run on-device through llama.cpp. Your files stay local; the model maps text queries and multimodal content into one ranking space.</p>
    </header>

    <article class="wide">
      <div class="callout" style="margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
        Model docs: <a href="https://ai.google.dev/gemma/docs/embeddinggemma" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google AI — EmbeddingGemma</a>. DigUp project: <a href="https://github.com/ARahim3/DigUp" rel="noopener noreferrer">ARahim3/DigUp</a>. Other apps (e.g. Aether) may also use EmbeddingGemma 2 — this page is DigUp-focused, not a competitor teardown.
      </div>

      <div class="cta-bar">
        <a class="cta" href="https://github.com/ARahim3/DigUp/releases" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Releases</a>
        <a class="cta-secondary" href="/what-is-digup/">What is DigUp?</a>
        <a class="cta-secondary" href="/install-mac/">Install Mac</a>
      </div>

      <h2>What EmbeddingGemma 2 is</h2>
      <p>EmbeddingGemma is Google’s embedding model family for turning inputs into vectors you can compare. DigUp uses <strong>EmbeddingGemma 2</strong> so a typed query can rank photos, PDF pages, document passages, audio windows, and video frames together — not only keyword hits.</p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Approximate scale cited in DigUp materials: ~740M parameters; ~865 MB download for DigUp’s quantized on-device package.</li>
        <li>Shared dimensionality commonly described as <strong>768-d</strong> for DigUp’s multimodal index.</li>
        <li>Runtime: <strong>llama.cpp</strong> (GGUF Q8-style weights), Metal on Mac; CPU or GPU (e.g. Vulkan) on Win/Linux after a speed check.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>One index, many modalities</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Input</th>
            <th>How DigUp uses the model</th>
            <th>You get</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Text query</strong></td>
            <td>Embed query with text tower at search time</td>
            <td>Nearest neighbors + keyword boost</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Images / screenshots</strong></td>
            <td>Visual embeddings (OCR text on Mac for scans/screenshots)</td>
            <td>Find by what’s depicted</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>PDF pages</strong></td>
            <td>Page-level meaning (+ text / OCR where available)</td>
            <td>Open to the right page</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Audio</strong></td>
            <td>~30s windows in the shared space</td>
            <td>Jump near the moment</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Video</strong></td>
            <td>Frame / moment embeddings</td>
            <td>Jump near the moment</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Local-first implications</h2>
      <ol class="checklist">
        <li><strong>First launch</strong> downloads the model (~865 MB). After that, indexing and search run on device.</li>
        <li><strong>No DigUp account</strong> — privacy model is local index + optional update check (can be disabled per upstream docs).</li>
        <li><strong>If network blocks the download (Mac 0.6.1)</strong> — Settings → Storage → Use Files… to supply the model files DigUp expects.</li>
        <li><strong>Hardware</strong> — Apple Silicon Metal on Mac; Win/Linux pick GPU when faster and equivalent, else CPU (CPU photo indexing is slower).</li>
      </ol>

